Ontological Manifold Hypothesis: Mathematical Foundations

Shared Constraint Manifolds for Embodied AGI — V4.1

Author: Ouadi Maakoul


Abstract

The Ontological Manifold Hypothesis (OMH) posits that sufficiently capable embodied intelligence systems converge not to a shared representation but to a shared constraint manifold — the set of states compatible with physics, causality, and embodiment. We provide the complete mathematical framework. The manifold $\mathcal{M}_R$ is defined as the viability kernel of a controlled dynamical system under typed constraints. The Manifold Compliance Metric (MCM) is a computable, PAC‑learnable scalar; a differentiable surrogate enables gradient‑based training. We prove Theorem 1 (Necessity) : any optimal agent in an irreversible environment must have MCM→0; a quantitative version gives an explicit bound linking return gap to MCM. Theorem 2 (Non‑uniqueness) constructs two optimal policies with zero MCM but non‑linearizable latent spaces. Theorem 3 (Gauge Equivalence) shows optimal agents are related by a principal bundle automorphism with a finite‑dimensional structure group over the task manifold. We specify a falsifiability criterion: OMH is refuted if competence increases without MCM decreasing. The theory is instantiated on the GDF humanoid, and a companion experimental paper is outlined. OMH thus stands as a testable, rigorous foundation for embodied AGI.

1. Introduction

The scaling of AI has revealed convergent representations across modalities (Platonic Representation Hypothesis, Huh et al. 2024), but rigorous null‑calibration shows only local relational structure survives (Aristotelian Representation Hypothesis, Brbić et al. 2026). Meanwhile, a theorem by Richens & Everitt (2024) proves that robust agents must learn the true causal model. These three results converge on a deeper principle: intelligence is not converging to a universal embedding, but to a shared constraint manifold. This paper formalizes that principle as the Ontological Manifold Hypothesis (OMH).

We move beyond conceptual claims. We build the mathematical machinery — viability kernel, typed constraints, a differentiable MCM, and three theorems — that renders OMH both falsifiable and optimizable. A central result is gauge equivalence: optimal embodied agents are related by internal coordinate transformations that leave physics projections invariant. This is the novel scientific contribution.

The theory builds on viability theory (Aubin 1991), which guarantees the existence of the viability kernel as the maximal invariant set under constraints. OMH contributes: (1) a computable, differentiable, and falsifiable scalar — the Manifold Compliance Metric (MCM) — grounded in a five‑type constraint taxonomy (kinematic, dynamic, energetic, morphological, causal); (2) the gauge‑equivalence principle relating optimal agents via principal bundle automorphisms, which is absent from viability theory; (3) the explicit incorporation of causal constraints via interventional distributions, which standard viability frameworks do not treat.

Falsifiability statement. OMH predicts that for any agent family with competence $J(\pi_\theta) \to J^*$, the Manifold Compliance Metric satisfies $\text{MCM}(\pi_\theta) \to 0$. If one demonstrates a sequence of agents with $J \to J^*$ but $\text{MCM} \not\to 0$, OMH is empirically falsified. A quantitative bound (Corollary 1.1) provides explicit numerical thresholds for finite‑sample tests.

