2. The Ontological Manifold

2.1 World State and Dynamics

Consider a continuous‑time dynamical system with state $x \in \mathcal{X} \subset \mathbb{R}^n$ and control $u \in \mathcal{U}(x) \subset \mathbb{R}^m$. The true dynamics are

\dot{x} = f(x,u,t),

where $f$ is locally Lipschitz in $x$, measurable in $t$, and continuous in $u$. The system is subject to a set of $K$ constraints, partitioned into five types (Table 1).

Table 1: Constraint Types

Class Form Units Example
Kinematic $\phi_j(q)=0$ (holonomic) rad, m joint loop closure
Dynamic $\tau_{\min} \le \tau_i \le \tau_{\max}$ N·m motor torque limits
Causal $p(x_i \mid \text{pa}_i)$ invariant under $\text{do}(\text{pa}_i)$ probability intervention invariance
Energetic $H(t) + \int_0^t (\dot{Q}_{\text{in}}-\dot{Q}_{\text{out}})ds \ge 0$, $\int_0^T \|u\|^2 dt \le E_{\text{budget}}$ J passivity, battery
Morphological $\text{sdf}(\text{body}_i, \text{obstacle}) \ge 0$ m collision avoidance

2.2 Viability Kernel Definition

Let $K = \{x \mid \text{all } g_i(x)=0,\, h_i(x) \ge 0\}$ be the constraint set. The viability kernel $\text{Viab}(K) \subseteq K$ is the largest closed subset of $K$ such that for every $x \in \text{Viab}(K)$, there exists a measurable control $u(\cdot)$ keeping $x(t) \in \text{Viab}(K)$ for all $t \ge 0$. Under regularity assumptions — $f$ Lipschitz, constraints $C^1$, $K$ closed with non‑empty interior — the viability kernel exists and is closed (Aubin 1991). The ontological manifold is

\mathcal{M}_R = \text{Viab}(K).

To ensure $\mathcal{M}_R \neq \emptyset$ for the GDF humanoid, we verify the tangential condition:

Lemma A.1 (Existence of viable controls). Let the constraint functions $g_i$, $h_j$ be $C^1$ and suppose their gradients are linearly independent on $\partial K$. Then $\text{Viab}(K) \neq \emptyset$ iff there exists $u \in \mathcal{U}(x)$ such that $f(x,u) \in T_K(x)$ for all $x \in \partial K$, where $T_K(x)$ is the contingent cone. For the PoE kinematics of the humanoid, the holonomic constraints $\phi_j(q)=0$ yield a full‑rank Jacobian except at singularities, so the condition holds away from a measure‑zero set. For torque, energy, and collision constraints, the control can always be chosen to respect the inequality boundaries, guaranteeing a non‑empty viability kernel.

This lemma connects the theory directly to the GDF architecture, confirming that the ontological manifold is non‑trivial.

