3. Manifold Compliance Metric (MCM)

3.1 Exact MCM

For a policy $\pi$ generating trajectory distribution $q_\pi$,

\text{MCM}(\pi) = \mathbb{E}_{\tau \sim q_\pi} \!\left[ \sum_{i=1}^K \text{CV}_i(\tau) \right]

with violations defined as integrals over time of constraint deviation:

\text{CV}_i(\tau) = \int_0^T \|g_i(x_t)\|\,dt \quad \text{or} \quad \int_0^T \max(0, -h_i(x_t))\,dt,

and for the causal term,

\text{CV}_{\text{causal}} = D_{\mathrm{KL}}\!\big( q_\pi(x_i \mid \text{pa}_i) \,\|\, p_{\text{true}}(x_i \mid \text{do}(\text{pa}_i)) \big).

Statistical guarantee. Because MDP trajectories are not i.i.d., we adopt a mixing‑time framework. Assume the policy induces a stationary distribution over trajectories that mixes within $H$ steps. By partitioning each episode into $m$ blocks of length $2H$ and discarding buffer blocks, we obtain $N$ effectively independent blocks. Let violations be bounded by $M$. Then with probability at least $1-\delta$,

\bigl| \widehat{\text{MCM}} - \text{MCM} \bigr| \le M \sqrt{\frac{\log(2/\delta)}{2N}}.

Thus to estimate MCM to accuracy $\varepsilon$ one needs $N = O(M^2 \log(1/\delta)/\varepsilon^2)$ blocks. For $M=10$, $\varepsilon=0.01$, $\delta=0.05$, this is approximately $2 \times 10^6$ blocks, which is feasible in simulation.

3.2 Differentiable Surrogate and Control Barrier Function Connection

To enable gradient‑based optimization, we replace $\max(0,-h)$ with the softplus barrier:

\widetilde{\text{CV}}_i(\tau) = \int_0^T \frac{1}{\alpha} \log\!\big(1 + \exp(-\alpha h_i(x_t))\big) \, dt,

with $\alpha > 0$ controlling sharpness. For equalities, $\widetilde{\text{CV}}_i = \int \|g_i(x_t)\|^2\,dt$ is smooth. The surrogate MCM $\widetilde{\text{MCM}}$ is differentiable w.r.t. policy parameters and can be used as a regularizer or penalty term in policy learning.

This construction is closely related to Control Barrier Functions (CBFs). A CBF $B(x)$ for a constraint $h(x) \ge 0$ must satisfy $\dot{B}(x) \le \gamma B(x)$ to guarantee forward invariance of the safe set. Here, taking $B(x) = \sum_i \phi_\alpha(h_i(x))$, we have

\dot{B} = \sum_i \phi_\alpha'(h_i) \nabla h_i \cdot f(x,u).

If a control policy minimizes $\widetilde{\text{MCM}}$ (thus driving $B$ low and $\dot{B}$ negative), then for a properly chosen $\alpha$ it enforces $h_i \ge -\alpha^{-1}\log(\text{const})$, ensuring practical forward invariance. The OMH framework therefore subsumes CBF‑based safe RL, but is distinct in that it unifies five physically grounded constraint types under a single metric and derives optimality guarantees (Theorem 1) beyond mere safety — it aligns optimality with manifold compliance as a necessary condition.

3.3 Falsifiability Criterion

Let $\{\pi_\theta\}$ be a family with task performance $J(\pi_\theta) \to J^*$. The OMH prediction is $\lim_{J \to J^*} \text{MCM}(\pi_\theta) = 0$. Moreover, from the quantitative Theorem 1 (Section 4.1), there exists a constant $c > 0$ such that for any policy $\pi$ with $J^* - J(\pi) \le \varepsilon$, we have $\text{MCM}(\pi) \le c \, \varepsilon$. If an empirical evaluation shows $\text{MCM}(\pi_\theta)$ bounded below by a positive constant while $J \to J^*$, OMH is rejected.

