4. Foundational Theorems

4.1 Theorem 1 (Necessity of Manifold Compliance)

Setup. Consider a discounted infinite‑horizon MDP with reward $r(x,u) \in [0,R_{\max}]$, discount $\gamma \in (0,1)$. Define the constraint‑violation set $F_\epsilon = \{x : \min_i h_i(x) < \epsilon\}$ for some $\epsilon > 0$. Assume that entering $F_0$ (the strict violation set) is absorbing: there exists $R_{\text{abs}} < J^*$ such that $\sup_\pi V^\pi(x) \le R_{\text{abs}}$ for any $x \in F_0$. Moreover, the dynamics are such that from any $x$ with $\min_i h_i(x) = \epsilon$ there is a positive drift toward larger violations, so that $F_\epsilon$ is a region of unavoidable cost.

Theorem 1 (Quantitative). Let $V^*(x_0)$ be the optimal value. For any policy $\pi$ with $V^\pi(x_0) \ge V^*(x_0) - \varepsilon$, the probability of entering $F_\epsilon$ is bounded by

P_\pi\bigl( \tau \text{ ever enters } F_\epsilon \bigr) \le \frac{\varepsilon}{ (R_{\max}/(1-\gamma) - R_{\text{abs}}) - \rho(\epsilon) },

where $\rho(\epsilon) \to 0$ as $\epsilon \to 0$. Consequently, the expected MCM satisfies

\text{MCM}(\pi) \le C \cdot P_\pi(\tau \text{ enters } F_\epsilon) + \epsilon T,

with $C$ a constant depending on maximum violation rates. As $V^\pi \to J^*$, both terms vanish, so $\text{MCM}(\pi) \to 0$.

Proof sketch. Define $F = F_\epsilon$. By the same decomposition as the original Theorem 1 but carefully bounding the value drop outside $F$. Let $\tau_F$ be the first entry time into $F$. For any path that avoids $F$, violations are at most $\epsilon$, hence MCM $\le \epsilon T$. For paths that enter $F$, the value suffers at least $\Delta = (R_{\max}/(1-\gamma) - R_{\text{abs}}) - \rho(\epsilon)$ due to the absorbing nature. The value gap $\varepsilon$ bounds $P(\tau_F < \infty) \le \varepsilon / \Delta$. The bound on MCM follows by splitting the expectation into entering and non‑entering trajectories. The link to the viability kernel is direct: an optimal policy must, with high probability, confine its state to $\text{Viab}(K)$, where $K$ is the set of constraints with margin $\epsilon$. $\Box$

4.2 Theorem 2 (Non‑uniqueness of Optimal Representations)

Construction. Consider a 2‑link planar arm with dynamics $M(q)\ddot{q} + C\dot{q} + g = \tau$, $|\tau_i| \le \tau_{\max}$. Let $\pi_1$ be a joint‑space PD+ controller, latent $z_1=(q,\dot{q})$. Let $\pi_2$ be a Port‑Hamiltonian controller with Hamiltonian $H = \frac{1}{2}p^T M_x^{-1}p + V(x_{\text{ee}})$, damping injection, latent $z_2=(x_{\text{ee}},p_{\text{ee}},H)$. Both policies track the same trajectory, satisfy all constraints ($\text{MCM}=0$), and achieve the same optimal return $J^*$. Yet there exists no linear map $W$ such that $W z_2 = z_1$ for all states, because the forward kinematics $x_{\text{ee}} = f(q)$ is nonlinear with non‑constant Jacobian. Hence internal representations are not globally linearly alignable, falsifying strong universal convergence while supporting OMH’s multiple‑chart view.

This example is not just a toy; the Port‑Hamiltonian controller directly builds on the energy‑shaping framework of Duong & Atanasov (2024) for Lie group dynamics, demonstrating that the structure arises naturally in modern robotics.

4.3 Theorem 3 (Gauge Equivalence of Optimal Agents)

Setup. Let $\mathcal{A}_1, \mathcal{A}_2$ be two agents with internal state spaces $Z_i$ and differentiable decoders $d_i: Z_i \to B$, where $B$ is the physical state base (e.g., SE(3) joint space modulo gauge symmetry). Let $\pi_i$ be optimal policies with $\text{MCM}=0$ and $J=J^*$ on a task submanifold $B_{\text{task}}$. Assume $B$ is a finite‑dimensional manifold and the fibers $F$ are finite‑dimensional vector spaces.

Theorem 3 (Gauge equivalence). Under the above assumptions, there exists a principal fiber bundle $(E, B, \pi_{\text{bdl}}, G)$ with total space $E$, projection $\pi_{\text{bdl}}: E \to B$, and a finite‑dimensional matrix Lie group $G \subset \text{GL}(n)$ acting on the fibers. The internal representations $Z_i$ are diffeomorphic to $E$, and there exists a bundle automorphism $\varphi: E \to E$ covering the identity on $B_{\text{task}}$ such that $d_2 \circ \varphi = d_1$ on the relevant fibers. Thus optimal agents are gauge‑equivalent: their physical predictions coincide, differing only by an internal coordinate transformation in $G$.

Proof sketch. Because both agents are optimal and MCM=0, the pushforward measures of their state processes onto $B_{\text{task}}$ must be identical (otherwise one would have a higher return). Since the task dynamics are deterministic and fully observable, the policy determines the distribution uniquely. By the Radon–Nikodym theorem, the densities of internal states are related by a measure‑preserving transformation. Because the decoders are smooth and the set of states is connected, this transformation is a diffeomorphism on each fiber. The collection of all such fiber diffeomorphisms forms a Lie group $G \subset \text{Diff}(F)$. By restricting to the connected component and noting that a finite‑dimensional approximation suffices for the task, we obtain a matrix Lie group. The bundle automorphism $\varphi$ is precisely the map that first applies the gauge transformation $g(x)$ on each fiber and then re‑assembles. $\Box$

Remark. The structure group $G$ captures the allowed internal transformations that leave physical observables unchanged. It is analogous to the gauge group in field theory, where internal symmetries do not affect observable physics. The finite‑dimensionality assumption is reasonable for practical robotic systems where the internal state is a vector of features of bounded dimension.

