5. Operationalizing the Causal Constraint

To make $\text{CV}_{\text{causal}}$ computable, we assume the agent has access to an approximate structural causal model (SCM) of the environment. The true conditional $p_{\text{true}}(x_i \mid \text{do}(\text{pa}_i))$ is estimated by one of three methods:

1. Controlled interventions: Physically executing $\text{do}(\text{pa}_i = a)$ and measuring resulting $x_i$.
2. Counterfactual simulation: Using a high‑fidelity simulator as a proxy.
3. Instrumental variable approximation: When interventions are impossible, using off‑policy data with known confounders.

We adopt the identifiability results of Ahuja et al. (2022), who show that under injective decoder and perfect single‑node interventions, the true interventional distribution can be identified up to a permutation and scaling. Accordingly, we define the causal MCM term using the estimated interventional distribution $\hat{p}$, and we bound the additional error by the total variation distance $\|\hat{p} - p_{\text{true}}\|$. This ensures that even with approximate causal knowledge, the MCM remains a valid measure of violation up to a known estimation bias.

In practice, the differentiable surrogate for $\text{CV}_{\text{causal}}$ is the empirical KL between the policy’s predicted conditional and the stored interventional histogram, encouraging the internal model to match causal invariances.

