Appendix B.0 — Existence of Viable Controls for the Typed Constraints (Lemma A.1)

Lemma B.1 (Tangential condition for typed constraints).
Let $K = \{x\in\mathcal{X} : g_i(x)=0\text{ for } i\in\mathcal{I}_e,\; h_j(x)\ge 0\text{ for } j\in\mathcal{I}_i\}$ with $g_i,h_j\in C^1$. Assume the gradients $\{\nabla g_i(x),\nabla h_j(x)\}_{j\in A(x)}$, where $A(x)=\{j:h_j(x)=0\}$, are linearly independent on $\partial K$. Then $\text{Viab}(K)\neq\emptyset$ iff for every $x\in\partial K$ there exists a control $u\in\mathcal{U}(x)$ such that $f(x,u)\in T_K(x)$, the contingent cone of $K$ at $x$.

Verification for the five OMH constraint classes:

1. Kinematic (holonomic) $\phi(q)=0$:
      The PoE kinematics give a full‑rank constraint Jacobian $\partial\phi/\partial q$ except at singularities. On regular points, $T_K(q)=\ker(\partial\phi/\partial q)$, and a control exists via the dynamically consistent pseudo‑inverse (Aubin, Ch. 4).
2. Dynamic (torque limits) $|\tau_i|\le\tau_{\max}$:
      Since $\tau_i$ are direct control inputs, one simply chooses $u_i$ within the bounds; no extra condition.
3. Energetic (battery) $E(t)\ge 0$, $\dot E = -\|u\|^2$:
      The set $E\ge 0$ is a half‑space. At $E=0$, the contingent cone requires $\dot E\ge 0$, which is only possible if $u=0$. The agent can always stop; thus the viability kernel includes states with $E=0$ and zero torque.
4. Morphological (collision) $\text{sdf}\ge 0$:
      At contact ($\text{sdf}=0$), the tangent cone requires $\nabla\text{sdf}\cdot f\ge 0$. A minimally‑invasive repulsive torque (barrier method) satisfies this.
5. Causal $D_\text{KL}(p_\pi\|p_{\text{true}})=0$:
      This is an informational constraint; it does not restrict the state space locally, so it contributes no boundary condition for the viability kernel.

Therefore, away from singularities and battery exhaustion, $\text{Viab}(K)$ is non‑empty for the GDF humanoid.

