Appendix B.1 — Full Proof of Theorem 1 (Quantitative Necessity)

Theorem 1 (Quantitative). Assume the environment has absorbing failure set $F_0=\{x:\min_j h_j(x)<0\}$ with $\sup_{x\in F_0}V^*(x)\le R_{\text{abs}}<J^*$, and a boundary layer $F_\epsilon=\{x:\min_j h_j(x)<\epsilon\}$ with $\epsilon>0$ such that from $F_\epsilon\setminus F_0$ there is a positive drift toward $F_0$: there exists $\delta>0$ so that for any $x\in F_\epsilon\setminus F_0$, the optimal policy cannot prevent entering $F_0$ with probability at least $\delta$ within a fixed horizon $H$.
Let $V^\pi(x_0)\ge V^*(x_0)-\varepsilon$. Then

P_\pi(\tau\text{ ever enters }F_\epsilon)\le \frac{\varepsilon}{R_{\max}/(1-\gamma)-R_{\text{abs}}-\rho(\epsilon)},


with $\rho(\epsilon)\to0$ as $\epsilon\to0$. Moreover,
\text{MCM}(\pi)\le C\,P_\pi(\tau\text{ enters }F_\epsilon)+\epsilon T,


where $C$ bounds the maximal violation integral if $F_0$ is entered.

Proof. Decompose the value using the first hitting time $\tau_\epsilon$ of $F_\epsilon$. For any trajectory that never enters $F_\epsilon$, violations are at most $\epsilon$ per step, giving the $\epsilon T$ term. For trajectories that do enter $F_\epsilon$, we compare with the optimal value. Let $p=P(\tau_\epsilon<\infty)$. By the absorption and drift assumptions, once in $F_\epsilon$, the expected future value is at most $R_{\text{abs}}+\rho(\epsilon)$, where $\rho$ captures the residual return inside the boundary layer before failure. The value identity then gives

V^\pi(x_0)\le \frac{R_{\max}}{1-\gamma}-p\left(\frac{R_{\max}}{1-\gamma}-R_{\text{abs}}-\rho(\epsilon)\right).

Since $V^\pi(x_0)\ge V^*(x_0)-\varepsilon\ge J^*-\varepsilon$, we solve for $p$, obtaining the bound. Finally, $\text{MCM}$ is bounded by $C\,p$ (for episodes that enter $F_0$, violations are bounded by $C=\max_\tau\sum_i\text{CV}_i(\tau)$) plus the contribution from non‑entering episodes ($\le\epsilon T$). As $\varepsilon\to0$, both terms vanish.

Connection to viability kernel. The optimal policy achieves $V^*$ by confining the state to $\text{Viab}(K)\subseteq K\setminus F_\epsilon$ (with margin). The regulation map $D(x)$ of $\text{Viab}(K)$ provides the necessary controls; an optimal policy must, with probability arbitrarily close to one, coincide with such controls. This completes the link between optimality and viability. $\square$

---

Appendix B.2 — Details of the Non‑Uniqueness Construction (Theorem 2)

The 2‑link arm is described in the main text. We emphasize the non‑linearizability: the forward kinematics $x_{\text{ee}}=f_{\text{kin}}(q)$ is nonlinear with Jacobian $J(q)$ not globally constant. Suppose there existed a linear map $W$ such that $W z_2 = z_1$ for all states on the task submanifold. Then $W (f_{\text{kin}}(q), J(q)\dot q, H) = (q,\dot q)$. Since $q\mapsto f_{\text{kin}}(q)$ is not injective (inverse kinematics has multiple solutions), no linear $W$ can recover $q$ from $x_{\text{ee}}$ uniquely. Hence the representations are not globally linear‑related.

---

Appendix C — Port‑Hamiltonian Model with ICNN Potential (Updated)

The Port‑Hamiltonian controller uses an Input‑Convex Neural Network (ICNN) for the potential $V(x_{\text{ee}})$ (Amos et al., 2017). The ICNN is defined by

V(x) = \beta\,x^Tx + \sum_{k}\sigma(W_k z_k + b_k),\quad z_{k+1} = \sigma(W'_k z_k + A_k x + b'_k)

with weight matrices constrained such that the network is convex in $x$. This guarantees that $V$ has a unique global minimum at the target, preserves $\dot H\le y^T u$ (passivity), and thus the Hamiltonian structure remains intact while learning the task. This addresses the concern that an unconstrained NN could break passivity. See Duong & Atanasov (2024) for a similar construction on Lie groups.

---

Appendix D — Differentiable MCM Surrogate as a Control Barrier Function

Define the softplus barrier $\phi_\alpha(h)=\frac1\alpha\log(1+e^{-\alpha h})$, $\alpha>0$. For a scalar inequality constraint $h(x)\ge0$, consider $B(x)=\phi_\alpha(h(x))$. Its time derivative along the dynamics is

\dot B(x) = \phi_\alpha'(h)\,\nabla h\cdot f(x,u) = -\frac{1}{1+e^{\alpha h}}\,\nabla h\cdot f.

If the control policy ensures $\dot B \le -\kappa B + \|u\|^2$ (e.g., by including $\dot B$ in the loss), then for $\kappa>0$ the set $\{x:B(x)\le b_0\}$ is forward invariant for sufficiently small $b_0$, implying $h\ge -\alpha^{-1}\log(e^{\alpha b_0}-1)$. As $\alpha\to\infty$, this recovers $h\ge0$ exactly. Hence minimizing the softplus MCM corresponds to learning a policy that renders the constraint set practically forward invariant, i.e., a Control Barrier Function. This links OMH to CBF‑based safe RL (Yang et al., 2025) while being distinct in its unified multi‑constraint, optimality‑driven framework.

