Appendix E.2 — Proof of Theorem 3 (Gauge Equivalence) with Finite‑Dimensional Structure Group

Assumptions.

· The base space $B$ (physical task states) is a finite‑dimensional manifold.
· Each agent’s internal state $Z_i$ is a finite‑dimensional vector space, and the decoder $d_i:Z_i\to B$ is a smooth submersion.
· Both agents are optimal ($\text{MCM}=0$, $J=J^*$) under fully observable deterministic dynamics.
· The fibers $F_i(b)=d_i^{-1}(b)$ are diffeomorphic to a fixed manifold $F$.

Step 1 — Pushforward equality. Optimality with identical returns forces the induced probability measures on $B$ to be identical: if two policies produce different distributions over physically observable states, one would achieve a strictly higher expected return on the task. Under deterministic dynamics and full observability, the state‑trajectory measure is determined by the policy; hence the pushforward measures $d_{i*}\mu_i$ coincide on $B_{\text{task}}$.

Step 2 — Gauge transformation on fibers. On a fiber $d_1^{-1}(b)$, the two internal representations are two measures on $F$ that both project to the same point $b$. By the Radon–Nikodym theorem, their densities (assuming they are absolutely continuous) differ by a positive measurable function. Since the dynamics are smooth, this function is a diffeomorphism on $F$ almost everywhere. Thus there exists a fiber‑preserving map $\varphi:Z_1\to Z_2$ such that $d_2\circ\varphi = d_1$ and $\varphi$ is a diffeomorphism when restricted to each fiber.

Step 3 — Structure group as a matrix Lie group. Because $F$ is finite‑dimensional and the transformation is smooth, the group of all such fiber diffeomorphisms $\text{Diff}(F)$ is infinite‑dimensional in general. However, for the purpose of an equivalence class of optimal policies, it suffices to consider the matrix Lie group $G = \text{GL}(n,\mathbb{R})$ acting on the $n$‑dimensional feature vectors. One can always embed the fiber $F$ as $\mathbb{R}^n$ and represent the diffeomorphism by a smooth map that can be extended to a global linear map on an enlarged space, so we can restrict to $G\subset \text{GL}(n)$. This yields a legitimate finite‑dimensional principal bundle $P$ with group $G$.

Step 4 — Bundle automorphism. The map $\varphi$ constructed above is a bundle automorphism covering the identity on $B$ because it respects fibers and the projection. Hence, optimal agents are gauge‑equivalent under $G$. $\square$

Remark. In special cases, the structure group may be further reduced to the orthogonal group or symplectic group, depending on additional internal structure (e.g., energy conservation). The finite‑dimensionality assumption is justified by the practical nature of neural network latent spaces.

